&#x200B;

A lot of people ask what we're actually trying to build.

&#x200B;

The answer is pretty simple.

&#x200B;

We love games.

&#x200B;

Not just modern games. Retro games. Arcade games. The weird stuff from the 70s, 80s, and 90s that was built by small teams with big ideas. Games that were fun because the gameplay was fun, not because they were designed to squeeze every dollar out of players.

&#x200B;

That's the spirit we're chasing.

&#x200B;

We also believe creators should embrace new technology. Not because it's trendy, but because every generation of game developers has used the best tools available to them.

&#x200B;

We use AI.

&#x200B;

We use modern game engines.

&#x200B;

We use blockchain technology.

&#x200B;

We experiment with cryptocurrency.

&#x200B;

Why? Because they're tools. Nothing more, nothing less.

&#x200B;

What we don't want to do is build pump-and-dump meme coins, sell overpriced JPEGs, or create systems where the entire goal is extracting money from players. The gaming industry already has enough of that.

&#x200B;

Our focus is creating games, communities, collectibles, competitions, and experiences that people genuinely enjoy participating in.

&#x200B;

If a technology helps make that experience better, we'll use it.

&#x200B;

If it doesn't, we won't.

&#x200B;

At the end of the day, we're just a bunch of gamers who grew up loving Asteroids, Galaga, Pac-Man, Missile Command, and thousands of other great games.

&#x200B;

The technology changed.

&#x200B;

The mission didn't.

&#x200B;

Build cool stuff.

Have fun.

Treat people fairly.

Leave the industry a little better than we found it
